#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:1
msgid "Error Type Compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:3
msgid ""
"When defining a custom error type, there are some common conventions to "
"follow in order to be compatible with the broader ecosystem:"
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:6
msgid ""
"Implement the standard library's [`Error`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/"
"std/error/trait.Error.html) trait."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:7
msgid "Derive `Debug` and implement `Display` (required by `Error`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:8
msgid ""
"Implement `From` conversions for underlying error types to support automatic "
"error type conversion with `?`."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:12
msgid "// Copyright 2023 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:13
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:30
msgid "\"I/O error: {e}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:31
msgid "\"Username file was empty\""
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:45
msgid ""
"Any type can be used as the error type for `Result`: There are no trait "
"requirements, structs and enums are both valid, and we can even use empty/"
"zero-sized types like `()` when the error doesn't need to cary any data."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:49
msgid ""
"While there aren't any strict requirements for error types, there are a "
"couple of conventions to follow for ecosystem compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:52
msgid ""
"We've already seen how the `From` trait can be used to support automatic "
"error type conversion with the `?` operator."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:55
msgid ""
"The standard library provides the `Error` marker trait for indicating that a "
"type represents an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:58
msgid ""
"`Error` depends on `Debug` (which can be derived) and `Display` (which must "
"be implemented manually). These ensure that an error can always be turned "
"into a string for the purpose of logging or displaying the error to a user."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:62
msgid ""
"The `Error` trait doesn't provide any functionality of its own, but in a "
"couple of slides we will look at how `Error` can be used to create dynamic "
"error types."
msgstr ""

#: src/error-handling/compatibility.md:66
msgid ""
"These trait implementations represent a bit of boilerplate that needs to be "
"written for each custom error type. On the next slide we'll look at a crate "
"in the ecosystem that can be used to cut down on this boilerplate."
msgstr ""
